🎯 The Mission: You type https://www.instagram.com and hit Enter

Let's walk through this like a movie with clear Acts 🎥

🎬 ACT 1: Browser Prep Work

Step 1: Parse the URL 🔍
Browser breaks down what you typed:

https://www.instagram.com
  ↳ Protocol: https
  ↳ Domain: www.instagram.com
  ↳ Path: / (homepage)

Step 2: Check caches first 🧠
Browser checks: "Do I already know instagram.com's IP address?"

Browser cache? → Check
OS cache? → Check
Router cache? → Check

(We covered this whole flow in DNS resolution!)

🎬 ACT 2: DNS Resolution — "Finding the address" 📇

If not cached, the full DNS chase begins:

Browser → Resolver (ISP) → Root Server → .com TLD Server → Instagram's Name Server
                                                              ↓
                                              Returns IP: 157.240.12.35 ✅

(Remember our whole "finding your gf's house" analogy? Exact same process here) 💌

🎬 ACT 3: TCP Connection — "Establishing trust" 🤝

Now the browser knows the IP. Time to actually CONNECT to that server.

3-Way Handshake:

Browser  ----SYN----->  Server
Browser  <--SYN-ACK---  Server
Browser  ----ACK----->  Server
       🎉 TCP Connection Established! 🎉

(Our "You up? 👀 → Yeah, you? 👀 → Bet 😎" analogy)

🎬 ACT 4: TLS/SSL Handshake — "Locking the box" 🔒

Since it's HTTPS, before any actual data is exchanged, we need encryption set up:

Browser: "Hey, let's talk securely, here's what I support"
Server:  "Cool, here's my SSL certificate to prove I'm really Instagram"
Browser: "Verified! ✅ Let's agree on a secret encryption key"
       🔐 Secure encrypted tunnel established! 🔐

(Our locked-box-letter analogy from before) 💌🔐

🎬 ACT 5: (Optional) Load Balancer + CDN routing 🌐

Before hitting Instagram's actual servers, the request might first pass through:

Load Balancer ⚖️ → decides WHICH backend server handles this request (Round Robin / Least Connections, remember?)
CDN 🚚 → if you're requesting a static asset (image, video, CSS), it might get served from the NEAREST edge server instead of traveling all the way to Instagram's main servers

(Our "McDonald's franchise" analogy — you get served from your nearest branch, not the one HQ) 🍔

🎬 ACT 6: HTTP Request — "Actually asking for the page" 📨

NOW, over that secure encrypted connection, the browser sends an actual HTTP GET request:

GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: www.instagram.com
Cookie: sessionId=abc123
User-Agent: Chrome/Windows

(Notice the Cookie header — this is how the server knows you're logged in! Remember our session/JWT breakdown?) 🍪

🎬 ACT 7: Server Processing 🖥️

The server receives the request and does its thing:

Checks authentication (is this a valid session/JWT?) 🔐
Queries the database if needed (fetch your feed, posts, etc.) 🗄️
Runs backend logic (Node.js/Django/whatever powers Instagram)
Prepares an HTML/JSON response
🎬 ACT 8: HTTP Response — "Sending the page back" 📬

Server sends back a response with a status code:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html

<html>...(actual page content)...</html>

(Remember our status code breakdown — 200 OK means "here's exactly what you asked for"!) ✅

🎬 ACT 9: Browser Renders the Page 🎨

Browser now:

Parses HTML → builds the DOM (page structure)
Parses CSS → applies styling
Executes JavaScript → makes it interactive
Fetches additional resources → images, fonts, videos (often via CDN, using MORE http requests for each!)

Each of these additional resources? They might repeat parts of this ENTIRE process (DNS lookup if different domain, TCP connection, etc.) — that's why a single webpage can trigger DOZENS of network requests happening in parallel! 🤯

🎬 ACT 10: Page Fully Loads 🎉

Instagram's feed appears on your screen. Total time elapsed: usually under 1-2 seconds, even though we just walked through like 10 major steps involving servers across the globe! ⚡